<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Chișinău</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Kishinev</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Chișinău, formerly Kishinev, is the capital and largest city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moldova">Moldova</a>. A small market town under the Principality of Moldavia, it became the administrative centre of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bessarabia">Bessarabia</a> after the Russian annexation of 1812 and grew rapidly in the 19th century; the pogrom of 1903 against its large Jewish community drew worldwide condemnation. In 1917 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sfatul-tarii">Sfatul Țării</a> met there and proclaimed the Moldavian Democratic Republic, and from 1991 it was the capital of independent Moldova.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/chisinau.jpg" alt="Chișinău" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Rodion Gavriloi · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Consiliul_Municipal_Chi%C8%99in%C4%83u_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Chișinău stands on the river Bâc, a tributary of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dniester">Dniester</a>, in the middle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moldova">Moldova</a>, on a relatively level landscape with very fertile ground. It is roughly equidistant between the borders with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romania">Romania</a>, 58 km away, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ukraine">Ukraine</a>, 54 km away, and very close to the geographic centre of the country. The city proper covers 120 km2 and the municipality 635 km2. Its climate is humid continental, with hot summers and cold, windy winters.</p><p>The origin of the name is unclear. One theory derives it from the archaic Romanian chișla, meaning spring or source of water, and nouă, new, because the town grew around a small spring. Another, attributed to the historian Ștefan Ciobanu, holds that it was formed like the name of Chișineu in western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romania">Romania</a>, from the Hungarian kis, small, and Jenő, one of the seven Hungarian tribes. A third, by the linguist Kiss Lajos, derives it from a Cuman word for a grave or kurgan.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Chișinău was founded in 1436 as a monastery village and belonged to the Principality of Moldavia, which from the 16th century was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> state of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a> while retaining its autonomy. At the beginning of the 19th century it was a small town of 7,000 inhabitants. In 1812, after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russo-turkish-war-1806-1812">Russo-Turkish War</a> of 1806-1812, the Ottomans ceded the eastern half of Moldavia to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-empire">Russian Empire</a>. The new territory became known as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bessarabia">Bessarabia</a>, and Chișinău became the capital of its oblast.</p><p>A generous development plan gave the city broad, long roads by 1834 and divided it roughly into an old part with irregular buildings and a newer centre and station. Between 1830 and 1836 the architect Avraam Melnikov built the Nativity Cathedral with a bell tower, and in 1840 the Triumphal Arch, planned by Luca Zaushkevich, was completed. The belfry was demolished in Soviet times and rebuilt in 1997. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-empire">Russian Empire</a> the city belonged to the Moldavian Democratic Republic in 1917-1918, to the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romania">Romania</a> in 1918-1940 and 1941-1944, and to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> in 1940-1941 and 1944-1991.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Chișinău has been the capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moldova">Moldova</a> since 1991. It is the country's main industrial and commercial centre and its largest transport hub, and the municipality holds nearly a third of the population. The city was significantly damaged in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> and by earthquakes. The architect Alexander Bernardazzi designed many buildings, including the City Hall and the Church of Saint Theodore, and much of the city is built of limestone quarried at Cricova. The house where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-pushkin">Alexander Pushkin</a> lived in exile is now a museum.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Chișinău” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>A regional council, the Sfatul Țării, met in <b>Chișinău</b> and on 2 December 1917 proclaimed the Moldavian Democratic Republic as an autonomous member of a planned Russian federation, with Ion Inculeț as president.</blockquote><a href="/state/moldavian-democratic-republic">Moldavian Democratic Republic</a></li></ul></section>
